#!RuleTest
#test CDISC-CG0034 expect=noViolation domain=LB
#note "S4(c), a MISSING USUBJID group key in both SV and the primary. The verdict rests on the owner rulings, not on CDISC text (USUBJID is Required, so a blank key is a data error another rule reports): GMK §4 point 1 (a missing value is a valid value of a variable and by default does not drop a group), GMK §4 point 4 and D11 grouping half (distinct missing markers and the empty string are distinct group keys), with operation-level group: shipping KEEP_MISSING_KEYS. So the SV row with blank USUBJID forms its own group {2}, and LB row 2 (blank USUBJID, VISITNUM=2) finds 2 in it -> silent. Both blank cells are the SAME kind (identical empty Char cells) on purpose: under D11 an empty string and a MissingValue marker are DIFFERENT groups. VISITNUM is populated in the blank-key group, because a group whose VISITNUMs are all missing resolves to the empty set. 2 is deliberately NOT in 01-701-1015's set. Catches a port that DROPS missing group keys: the lookup falls to the declared empty-set default and row 2 fires."
#library standard=sdtmig version=3-4
dataset LB class=FINDINGS label="Laboratory Test Results"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col LBSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col LBTESTCD type=Char label="Lab Test or Examination Short Name"
col VISITNUM type=Num  label="Visit Number"
col VISIT    type=Char label="Visit Name"
---
CDISCPILOT01 | LB | 01-701-1015 | 1 | ALB | 1 | SCREENING
CDISCPILOT01 | LB |             | 1 | ALB | 2 | BASELINE
---

dataset SV label="Subject Visits"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col VISITNUM type=Num  label="Visit Number"
col VISIT    type=Char label="Visit Name"
---
CDISCPILOT01 | SV | 01-701-1015 | 1 | SCREENING
CDISCPILOT01 | SV |             | 2 | BASELINE
---
